      "question": "Give me a short comparison of AMD's FY2024 and FY2025 manufacturing-dependence and export-control risks. What actually changed, what is mostly a repeated warning, and does later reporting show those risks easing or becoming more concrete? Keep the filed comparison separate from the later context and flag anything the evidence cannot settle.",
      "answer": "- **Annual filing comparison:** FY2024 ended December 28, 2024; FY2025 ended December 27, 2025. Original 10-Ks filed February 5, 2025, and February 4, 2026, respectively—not the later amendment.\n\n  - **Export controls—substantively more concrete.** FY2024 already warned that licenses could block shipments, including products below existing performance thresholds. FY2025 reports the April 2025 MI308 restriction, inventory consequences, subsequent licenses and fourth-quarter shipments. This is realization and partial mitigation of an existing exposure, not an entirely new risk. China import rules, customer demand and licensing still condition sales. The filing also adds an unformalized U.S. revenue-sharing expectation. [S264][S328]\n\n  - **Manufacturing dependence—mostly repeated warnings.** The TSMC capacity warning is identical: insufficient advanced-node production could force customer allocations and lost sales. Repetition does not establish either deterioration or relief. [S31][S32]\n\n  - **Materials supply—meaningful update.** FY2025 adds an actual industry-wide memory shortage to the existing limited-supplier warning. Manufacturing-input exposure became more concrete; the underlying dependence was already disclosed. [S236][S237]\n\n- **Separate later reporting—after February 4, 2026:**\n  - **Exports:** Reuters’ February 26 report describes permission to resume MI308 shipments as a **2025 development**, not a new post-filing easing. It says DeepSeek’s purchase approval remained unclear. This supports selective relief, not unrestricted China access. [W1]\n  - **Manufacturing and memory:** CNBC reported continuing industry memory, manufacturing and advanced-packaging constraints on May 5. October 9 reporting described broader Samsung supply discussions while noting Lisa Su’s acknowledgment of tight memory supply. These indicate continuing exposure and potential mitigation—not proof that additional qualified capacity was delivered. [W13][W12]\n\n- **Evidence limits:** These excerpts cannot establish AMD-specific lost production, sufficient future capacity, durable China approvals or final revenue-sharing terms. Industry shortages do not prove AMD suffered a particular disruption; partnership discussions do not establish an all-clear. Later reporting does not change the filed classifications.",
