    {
      "id": "date_validation_qcom_customer_modems",
      "variant": "both",
      "companies": [
        "QCOM"
      ],
      "years": [
        2024,
        2025
      ],
      "question": "How did Qualcomm's customer-concentration and customer-designed-chip risks change from FY2024 to FY2025? Give me a concise risk brief that distinguishes a stronger warning from a new exposure, then connect the comparison to relevant later developments involving major handset customers. Make clear which effects are reported facts and which remain expectations.",
      "answer": "- **Main change: stronger warnings, not a newly disclosed exposure.** FY2024 already expected Apple to replace Qualcomm modems in some or all future devices. FY2025 says Apple will **increasingly** use its own modems and that this **will have a significant negative impact** on QCT revenue, operating results and cash flows. It also names Xiaomi alongside Apple and Samsung as customer-chip developers. This makes an existing risk more explicit and consequential; naming Xiaomi does not establish that customer-designed chips became a new economic exposure. [S5] [S6]\n\n- **Customer concentration remains structurally similar.** Both years warn about dependence on a few premium-handset customers, their purchasing power, launch timing and development spending without purchase commitments. FY2025 adds the stronger Apple substitution warning directly to this discussion. It distinguishes two mechanisms: lower Qualcomm content when Apple devices using Qualcomm thin modems gain share, and lost modem business when Apple substitutes its own chips. [S3] [S4]\n\n- **Later reported effects versus expectations:**\n  - **October 7, 2026:** Zacks reporting carried by *The Globe and Mail* described a realized decline in Qualcomm’s fiscal-third-quarter handset revenue. Separately, it attributed to management expectations of lower Apple modem share and further Apple-product revenue declines. Those expectations are not completed losses, and the reported overall handset decline does not isolate Apple substitution as its cause. [W9]\n  - **September 20, 2026:** BigGo reported teardown evidence that the U.S. iPhone 18 Pro Max retained a Qualcomm modem, while describing Apple-modem use in other models. This supports partial displacement—not a completed customer exit. [W5]\n  - **September 28, 2026:** *Cult of Mac*, citing Bloomberg reporting, described an expectation that all 2027 iPhones would use Apple modems. That remains a future scenario, not an accomplished transition. The returned evidence did not establish a specific, dated post-filing Samsung or Xiaomi substitution event. [W1]\n\n- **Comparison basis:** Original 10-Ks for FY2024 (September 25, 2023–September 29, 2024), filed November 6, 2024, and FY2025 (September 30, 2024–September 28, 2025), filed November 5, 2025. Later developments above occurred after the newer filing; they do not change the filing-language classifications. [S3] [S4]",
